A||||6||Fret Naught||Steering Colume woes revisisted||||11||
Z||000000||Fret Naught||01-14-2001||08:50 PM||||OOPS!!!<BR>Took the car out for a ride today, and noticed that my turn signals didn't cancel<BR>then I put on the right blinker and turned left guess what they canceled. Got home and took a closer look WHAT THE !!!!! the steering wheel is 180 degrees out. Every thing is keyed, how could that have happened. then it dawned on me, when I had the tilt unit apart, that nylon knuckel came apart, and I went ahead and cleaned it up re greased it, and put it back in, must have put it in 180 out.<P>Is there a quick and easy way to fix this, say disconnect the shaft or steering somewhere where it isn't keyed and spin it around? I know one thing for sure after yesterday, I am not taking that or any other tilt unit apart again!!! I will either live with this or find another colum in a junk yard.<BR>Thanks<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FretNaught_sig_19.gif"> ||||reg||11
Z||000001||theogre||01-14-2001||11:39 PM||||Nope. the entire column shaft is keyed. take it back apart.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fret Naught||01-16-2001||12:56 AM||||All done, didn't have to take it al down either, I just removed the spring plate, and re clocked it 180 degrees out and wa laa works good last long time!!!!<BR>bout time something went in my favor<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FretNaught_sig_21.gif"> <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000003||Songman||01-16-2001||11:29 AM||||Any easy way to tighten up the tilt? I know this has been talked about... but surely there is a way other than taking it all apart!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fret Naught||01-16-2001||12:42 PM||||Sorry, but I don't think so, the easiest way would be go to a junk yard find a nice tight one, bring it home and swap them. I heard some one say they found one for 40 bucks, It is worth every dime. In my most humble opinion.||||reg||9
Z||000005||87ECO5||01-16-2001||12:54 PM||||<B>Songman</B> - if you go the route of replacing the existing column with a "new" one, make sure you use a column with the same options <B>AND</B> from the same year.<P>I replaced the delay wiper/tilt/manual tranny column in my '87 with a delay wiper/tilt/manual tranny from an '84 ... NOW MY WIPERS DON'T WORK ON ANY SETTING EXCEPT THE MIST SETTING!||||reg||4
Z||000006||PAUL88GT||01-16-2001||01:28 PM||||I agree with you, Fret Naught. In my area, good used ones cost $60-65. They are not too difficult to install. I recently installed one in my 87 Trans Am. Their internal workings are somewhat complicated. Best of luck.<BR>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by PAUL88GT (edited 01-16-2001).]||||reg||1
